        "title": "Watchlist > toggles watched when the checkbox is clicked",
        "kind": "modified",
        "reason": "Step 1 verification (no component/template defect): the template passes each row's own movie.id - not a loop index - to (change)=\"toggleWatched(movie.id)\", and the bubbled row click is a no-op because onRowClick returns early when the target is inside 'a, button, input', so a checkbox click toggles exactly one movie exactly once (no double-toggle/net no-op). I added a test with unwatched ids 10/20/30: clicking the checkbox on the LAST rendered row marks only Movie 30 watched, and another test confirms the toggled movie re-orders behind the remaining unwatched movies. The failure is therefore purely positional, exactly the case you described: with ids 1 and 2 both unwatched the rows render [Movie 1, Movie 2]; after the click Movie 1 is watched and re-renders behind the still-unwatched Movie 2 as [Movie 2, Movie 1], so the old assertion `checkbox().checked` re-queried the FIRST rendered row - Movie 2's unchecked checkbox - yielding 'expected false to be true'. The store-level assertions in the same test (watchedCount() === 1, subtitle '1 of 2 watched') still pass, proving the toggle happened once. Requested minimal, intent-preserving update: the same real DOM click on the checkbox is kept, the watchedCount and subtitle assertions are kept verbatim, and the only change is that the clicked movie's checkbox is found by its stable title (checkboxFor('Movie 1')) instead of by list position. Nothing was weakened or removed - the assertion is now strictly stronger since it pins the toggled movie's identity.",
        "round": 2
      },
